    "text": "Emrullah Isler, Turkiye’s Ambassador to Saudi Arabia, stated that Turkiye stands with the Kingdom in the face of what he described as unlawful and inhumane attacks by the Houthi militia. These statements follow multiple attacks on Saudi Arabia, including an October 10 missile and drone strike on King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh that killed at least 13 people and injured dozens, and a recent attack on King Fahd International Airport in Dammam that killed two people. In response to the wave of attacks by the Houthis, Egypt’s parliament on October 26, 2026, authorized President Abdel Fattah el-Sisi to provide military assistance to defend Saudi Arabia if requested. The decision passed with a two-thirds majority in the House of Representatives, allowing el-Sisi, in coordination with Riyadh, to take measures to protect Saudi territory, sovereignty, and holy sites. The Egyptian parliament's statement did not specify the type of assistance or announce the deployment of troops. President el-Sisi previously stated that any Egyptian military role would be limited to defending Saudi territory and borders, ruling out attacks on Yemen. On October 17, Yahya al-Raei, speaker of the Houthi-controlled parliament in Sanaa, warned Egypt that military involvement would endanger Egypt’s own security and stability."
