{
  "headline": "Study finds over 45% of surveyed women in Bangladesh face technology-facilitated gender-based violence",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A study launched on October 29, 2026, reported that approximately 45.5 percent of surveyed women in Bangladesh faced technology-facilitated gender-based violence (TFGBV). According to the findings, 34.2 percent of respondents experienced two or more overlapping forms of abuse, while 14.8 percent reported three or more. The research, titled “Abuse Beyond the Screen: A Study on Technology-Facilitated Gender-Based Violence in Bangladesh,” was jointly conducted by Bangladesh Mahila Parishad and Digitally Right Limited with support from the Embassy of Sweden. The results were presented in Dhaka by Abdullah Titir, head of research at Digitally Right Limited, and Afroja Arman, senior training and research officer at Bangladesh Mahila Parishad. The study also cited 2025 data from the Bangladesh Bureau of Statistics (BBS) and UNFPA, which indicated that 8.3 percent of women surveyed had experienced TFGBV at some point in their lives."
    },
    {
      "text": "The mixed-method study was conducted from January 7 to 13, surveying 521 women and girls aged 15 and above across Dhaka, Chattogram, Barishal, Rajshahi, and Sylhet. The respondent group included four key-informant interviews and eight in-depth interviews with survivors. Age demographics included those aged 15 to 24 (29 percent), 25 to 34 (27.8 percent), 35 to 44 (20.3 percent), 45 to 54 (14.8 percent), and 55 and above (8.1 percent). Among active internet users surveyed, 63.7 percent reported experiencing TFGBV, with 57.4 percent reporting such violence within the previous 12 months. The study noted that 67 percent of the 71 percent of respondents who were active internet users had never received formal online safety guidance or training."
    },
    {
      "text": "The study identified specific types of abuse: harassment and gender-based abuse affected 52.7 percent, economic exploitation and fraud affected 37.1 percent, sexual and image-based abuse affected 24.5 percent, and identity misuse or abuse involving fake IDs affected 17.3 percent. Vulnerability varied by demographic, with 49 percent of respondents aged 15 to 24 reporting TFGBV and 76.7 percent of university-educated women reporting the violence."
    }
  ]
}

