  "headline": "Tokyo consumer inflation rises 2.6% in October",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Consumer prices in Tokyo rose 2.6% in October from a year earlier, according to data reported by the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications on Friday. This figure, which excludes fresh food, remains above the Bank of Japan’s 2% target and follows a 2.7% increase recorded in September. The 2.6% reading matched the median forecast from economists, who had predicted 2.7% gains for both the core and underlying measures. Overall inflation in the capital was reported at 2.8%, while prices excluding both fresh food and energy climbed 2.6%. The inflation data in Tokyo serves as a leading indicator for nationwide trends, though the relationship can be distorted by localized measures to cap living costs. The report was released hours before the Bank of Japan's board is expected to hold its policy settings steady."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "BNP candidate Mirza Faisal Amin wins Thakurgaon-1 by-election in Bangladesh",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Mirza Faisal Amin, a candidate for the Bangladesh Nationalist Party (BNP), won the Thakurgaon-1 by-election, defeating the closest rival, Jamaat-e-Islami candidate Delawar Hosen, by 131,336 votes. Kamrul Islam, the Returning Officer and Thakurgaon District Election Officer, declared Faisal the winner around 9:30 p.m. on Thursday. Faisal, who is the younger brother of BNP Secretary General Mirza Fakhrul Islam Alamgir, received 227,789 votes, while Hosen secured 96,453 votes. Voter turnout for the by-election was 64.77 percent. Election and administrative officials from various levels of the district were present during the announcement of the results."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "European Central Bank President Christine Lagarde to step down in March",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Christine Lagarde, the president of the European Central Bank (ECB), has announced she will step down from her role in March, nine months earlier than her scheduled October departure. Lagarde stated her decision is driven by a desire for freedom of speech and expression, noting that the rules of neutrality governing her position prevent her from influencing debates regarding financial and economic affairs or engaging on matters outside of monetary policy. She expressed a wish to confront the “misrepresentation of the truth.”<br><br>Lagarde, a former managing director of the International Monetary Fund and former finance minister of France, made the announcement at the conclusion of a press conference regarding the ECB's decision to maintain its key deposit rate at 2.5%, despite inflation reaching 3.8% last month—the highest level since September 2023. <br><br>The European Council, composed of EU heads of state, is responsible for appointing the head of the ECB. European Council President Antonio Costa has stated that the process to find a successor is underway. Potential candidates mentioned include former Dutch central bank president Klaas Knot, Spanish banker Pablo Hernández de Cos, German central bank president Joachim Nagel, and Nadia Calviño, the president of the European Investment Bank. <br><br>Speculation has arisen regarding Lagarde's future roles, including her being tipped as a candidate to lead the World Economic Forum. Additionally, there has been speculation that French President Emmanuel Macron may have encouraged her early departure to ensure a successor is appointed before potential shifts in the French presidency, though this remains unconfirmed. Lagarde will also be eligible to participate in elections next April."
    }
  ]
}
